<description>
Move every foreground object straight downward by a number of rows equal to its own pixel count.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- The foreground is made of separated 4-connected monochromatic objects.
- Objects may move partly below the bottom edge of the grid.

Rule:
- Clear the canvas to the background.
- For each foreground object, compute its area in pixels.
- Redraw the object at the same columns and that many rows lower, clipping pixels below the grid.

Key insight:
- The downward displacement is the object's own area; color and shape only identify which pixels move together.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "4-connected monochromatic foreground objects",
  "path": "vertical downward translation by the object's own pixel count",
  "trajectory": "translate the whole object without changing its shape; clip pixels below the bottom edge",
  "transform": "clear original object positions to the background"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10 [observed]
  background: 7 (orange) [observed]
  objects:
    count: 3 [observed]
    colors: 3 distinct non-bg colors, one per object; {2,5,9} [observed]
    connectivity: 4-connected monochromatic
    area: 1-5 per object
    shape: arbitrary 4-connected polyomino within bounding box
  placement:
    no overlap in input
    row 0 is all background [observed]
    topmost object starts at row >= 1 [observed]
  rule: each object translates down by exactly its own pixel count (area)
  output_constraints:
    moved objects must not overlap each other
    at least 1 pixel per object remains visible (not fully clipped below grid)
    partial clipping allowed (0-2 objects may clip) [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid 10x10, bg=7, choose 3 distinct non-bg colors.
    2. Generate 3 random 4-connected polyominoes with areas in [1..5].
    3. Place them in the OUTPUT grid at their final positions, ensuring no overlap.
       Constraint -- for each object with area A placed at rows [r_min..r_max],
       the INPUT position is A rows higher, so r_min - A >= 1 (row 0 stays bg).
       Also at least 1 pixel must remain visible, so r_min < H (always true if placed in grid).
    4. Derive INPUT by shifting each object UP by its area. Objects that partially
       clip in output have their input position closer to the bottom -- this is fine.
    5. Verify no overlap in the input grid (reject if collision).
    6. Optionally allow 0-2 objects to partially clip off the bottom in output
       (place output position so max_row + 1 > H for some pixels). Ensure at least
       1 pixel of each object remains visible in output (i.e., min_row < H).
```
